<!-- ============ EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="exec">Executive Summary</h1>
<p>This section is a plain-language overview of the Grievance Management Module. Sections 1 onward add the technical layer &ndash; architecture, entities, API domains, Figma-verified screens, RBAC, and full traceability to the FRS/Scope documents &ndash; without the column-level schema or full endpoint catalogue an engineering handoff would eventually need.</p>

<h2>What the Module Does</h2>
<p>The module gives farmers, cooperatives, FPOs, NGOs, and Woreda/Kebele bodies a formal way to raise a grievance about an OAN-enabled agricultural service, and gives ATI, MoA, and partner departments a structured way to receive, act on, and close that grievance &ndash; with every step tracked and every submitter kept informed. It supports several ways of reaching the system (mobile app, web portal, IVR/helpline, call-centre or Development-Agent assisted entry, and Telegram), and it never resolves a grievance itself &ndash; every outcome is a decision made by a person in a responsible department.</p>

<h2>The Three Core Workflows</h2>
<h3>Submission and Routing</h3>
<pre class="diagram">Submitter authenticates (Fayda ID / OTP / Officer ID)
        │
        ▼
Selects a channel (mobile app, web portal, IVR, call centre, or Development-Agent assisted)
        │
        ▼
Completes the form (submitter type, category, grievance type, description, evidence)
        │
        ▼
System validates the form and checks for a likely duplicate
        │
        ▼
Ticket number generated, status set to "Submitted", submitter acknowledged by SMS/email
        │
        ▼
Routing rules checked ──── match found ────► Assigned to department, department notified
        │
        └──── no match ────► Placed in the Nodal Officer's manual queue
                              (stays "Submitted" until a person assigns it)
</pre>

<h3>Department Response and Resolution</h3>
<pre class="diagram">Department notified of a new assignment; response-time clock starts
        │
        ▼
Officer opens the case, status moves to "In Progress"; submitter notified
        │
        ▼
Officer investigates; may request more information (status → "More Info Needed")
        │
        ▼
Officer submits a structured response (action taken, findings, proposed closure date)
        │
        ▼
Submitter notified with the full response; status moves to "Pending Submitter";
a confirmation window opens (7 days by default)
        │
        ├── Submitter confirms ──► "Resolved" → "Closed"; satisfaction rating requested
        ├── Submitter reopens (with reason) ──► back to "In Progress"
        └── No response in time ──► auto-closed as "no objection received"
</pre>

<h3>SLA Monitoring and Escalation</h3>
<pre class="diagram">Response-time clock running against the category's configured SLA
        │
        ├── 50% elapsed ──► reminder to the assigned officer
        ├── 80% elapsed ──► second reminder; Nodal Officer sees an at-risk report
        ├── SLA breached (100%) ──► "Escalated" flag set, priority raised, department head
        │                           and Nodal Officer notified
        ├── 2× SLA breached ──► escalated further to a senior authority
        └── Submitter may also manually escalate at any point once the SLA has passed
             (same notifications as a breach, marked as submitter-triggered)

Escalation clears automatically once the department submits its structured response.
Escalated and Anonymized are independent flags &ndash; neither ever replaces the status above.
</pre>

<h2>What a User Sees: Portal Screens</h2>
<p>Confirmed against the actual Figma prototype (full detail in &sect;4):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Screen</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Operational KPIs (Total, Awaiting Action, Resolved, Escalated), trends, recent grievances, escalation alert</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>All Grievances</td><td>Searchable, filterable work queue grouped by status</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grievance Detail</td><td>Full case view: communication thread, department responses, internal notes, SLA tracker, case management, submitter details</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Submit Grievance</td><td>A three-step guided form (Submitter Identity &rarr; Grievance Details &rarr; Review &amp; Submit)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Analytics &amp; Reporting</td><td>Performance metrics: resolution time, resolution rate, escalation rate, satisfaction, regional/category breakdowns</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>User Management</td><td>Add/deactivate users, assign roles and regions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Administration</td><td>Nodal Officers (L1/L2), Category Assignments, SLA Configuration, Notification Config, Response Templates</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Who Can Do What</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Role</th><th>Can see / do</th></tr>
<tr><td>Farmer / direct submitter</td><td>Only their own grievances: submit, view, respond to information requests, confirm or reopen a resolution, escalate after the SLA has passed</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Development Agent (assisted submissions)</td><td>Grievances they submitted on behalf of a farmer; the farmer remains the owner, the officer's identity is kept for audit</td></tr>
<tr><td>L1 Nodal Officer</td><td>Cases assigned within their category/department/region: review, update status, respond, request reassignment</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>L2 Senior Nodal Officer</td><td>Same as above, plus approving reassignment requests and SLA deferral requests</td></tr>
<tr><td>Department Head</td><td>Oversight of their department's cases and SLA settings</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>OAN Administrator (ATI)</td><td>Full administrative access: users, routing rules, SLA policy, notification templates, and audit records (view only &ndash; never delete)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The system defaults to denying access; a user must have an active account, the right role, and the case (or an approved administrative scope) explicitly assigned to them before any action is allowed.</p>

<!-- ============ 1. ARCHITECTURE ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s1">1. Architecture Overview</h1>
<p>The module sits between submitters and institutional actors, and depends on a small set of external systems for identity and communication.</p>
<pre class="diagram">┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                CHANNEL LAYER                                   │
│   Mobile App (Farmer/DA) | oan_grievance_ui (Officer/Admin website) |         │
│   IVR/Helpline | Call Centre | Telegram Bot                                    │
└───────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                     ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  API GATEWAY &ndash; AuthN (GEN2 Registry OAuth/SSO for Farmer/DA, Officer ID for  │
│  staff) &middot; RBAC pre-check &middot; rate limiting                                    │
└──────────────┬───────────────┬───────────────┬───────────────┬────────────────┘
               ▼               ▼               ▼               ▼
      Identity & RBAC   Grievance Svc   Response/Workflow   Routing &
      (auth, permissions) (submission,   Svc (structured     Reassignment Svc
                          lifecycle)     responses)
               │               │               │               │
               └───────────────┴───────┬───────┴───────────────┘
                                        ▼
                          DOMAIN EVENT BUS  (status changes, responses,
                          escalations, reassignments)
                          │            │            │
                   Notification   Audit Service   SLA/Escalation
                   Service        (append-only)   Scheduler
                                        │
                                        ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│   DATA LAYER: MariaDB 11.8 (OLTP) &middot; Object Store (attachments) &middot; Redis &middot; Queue │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                        │
                                        ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  INTEGRATIONS: GEN2 Registry (Farmer OAuth/DA SSO) &middot; Beneficiary &middot; Fayda ID &middot;  │
│  SMS &middot; Email &middot; IVR &middot; Telegram &middot; ATI/MoA Systems &middot; BI Export              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
<p>Services are logically separated so each can be scaled and released independently; whether they are separate deployables or modules within one service is an implementation decision for Development Milestone 1, not fixed here. Confirmed stack (from the actual running bench and repos, not a recommendation): Next.js + TypeScript (portal, per <code>Grievance-Management-Project</code>), Frappe (Python) backend services (per <code>oan_grievance_service</code>), MariaDB 11.8, Redis (cache + queue), and an offline-first Android app with local write-ahead sync.</p>
<p><b>Two design choices worth calling out:</b> (1) every channel (mobile, web, IVR, DA-assisted, Telegram) submits through the <i>same</i> grievance API &ndash; channel is a recorded attribute, not a separate code path; (2) <code>status</code>, <code>escalated</code>, and <code>anonymized</code> are independent fields, never combined into compound statuses, matching the FRS's own rule that Escalated is an overlay, not a status.</p>

<!-- ============ 2. FUNCTIONAL DESIGN SUMMARY ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s2">2. Functional Design Summary (FR-01&ndash;FR-11)</h1>
<table>
<tr><th>Area</th><th>Design Approach</th></tr>
<tr><td>Identity &amp; RBAC (FR-01)</td><td>Deny-by-default: every action is checked against role, active status, assigned case, and region/department/category scope. Routing a case to a department never by itself grants edit rights &ndash; an explicit assignment or approval permission is required. Every check (allow or deny) is logged.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Submission (FR-02)</td><td>One channel-agnostic submission path; the form adapts to submitter type and category. Ticket numbers follow REGION-WOREDA-CATEGORY-SEQUENCE. Duplicate detection compares identity + grievance type + a time window and warns rather than blocks. Mobile offline sync uses a client-generated idempotency key so a retried sync can't create a duplicate ticket.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Routing (FR-03)</td><td>Rule-based match on category/type/area/provider; no match falls to the Nodal Officer's manual queue rather than blocking the submission.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Reassignment (&sect;3.3.1 FRS)</td><td>An L1 officer's reassignment request is pending until an L2 officer approves it; the target department has no rights until that approval commits. Every request/decision is an immutable audit entry.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lifecycle (FR-04)</td><td>Single canonical status per grievance (see Executive Summary diagram); Escalated and Anonymized are independent overlay flags. Reopen requires a mandatory reason.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Department Response (FR-05)</td><td>A structured response (action taken, resolution summary, proposed closure date, response type) is appended immutably; response type drives the next status: Resolved/Partially Resolved &rarr; Pending Submitter, Referred &rarr; back to Assigned, Requires Further Info &rarr; More Info Needed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Submitter Confirmation (FR-06)</td><td>A 7-day (default) confirmation window; confirm &rarr; Resolved &rarr; Closed; reopen &rarr; back to In Progress with a mandatory reason; no response &rarr; auto-closed.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SLA &amp; Escalation (FR-07)</td><td>Per-category SLA clock; reminders at 50%/80%; escalation at 100% (department head + Nodal Officer notified); second-level escalation at 2&times; SLA to a senior authority &ndash; a separate configuration value from the 100% threshold. Deferral requests need L2 approval unless policy allows self-approval.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notifications (FR-08)</td><td>Every status change, response, and escalation is a domain event consumed by a Notification Service &ndash; the core services never call SMS/email APIs directly. Bilingual (Amharic/English). Rapid successive events are batched to avoid redundant messages.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Reporting (FR-09)</td><td>Dashboard (operational) and Analytics &amp; Reporting (performance/trend) are two distinct screens (confirmed in Figma), both reading from reporting projections rather than live OLTP queries, so totals reconcile and stay fast at scale.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audit (FR-10)</td><td>Status history, response records, and access decisions are append-only &ndash; no application-level update or delete path. Corrections are new rows, never edits.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Portal (FR-11)</td><td>See &sect;4 &ndash; verified against the real Figma prototype.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============ 3. DATA & API ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s3">3. Data Entities and API Domains</h1>
<h2>3.1 Core Entities</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Entity</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td>grievances</td><td>Current-state record of each case: submitter, category, status, priority, assignment, SLA due date, escalated/anonymized flags</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>submitter_profiles</td><td>Identity + type-specific fields per submitter type (farmer, cooperative/FPO, NGO, Woreda/Kebele body, Development Agent)</td></tr>
<tr><td>grievance_responses</td><td>Every structured department response &ndash; append-only, sequentially numbered per case</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>status_history / escalation_log / access_audit_event</td><td>Append-only audit trail of every status change, escalation, and access decision</td></tr>
<tr><td>routing_rules / departments</td><td>Category/type/area &rarr; department mapping; department directory</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>sla_configurations</td><td>Per-category SLA days, max deferral, first-level (100%) and second-level (2&times;) escalation thresholds &ndash; kept as two distinct fields, confirmed against the Figma's SLA Configuration screen</td></tr>
<tr><td>notification_log</td><td>Every dispatched notification: event, recipient, channel, status</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>rbac_assignments</td><td>User &rarr; role + region/department/category scope, effective-dated</td></tr>
<tr><td>reassignment_requests</td><td>Reassignment/deferral request-approval workflow state</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>attachments</td><td>Files linked to a grievance or response, virus-scanned on upload</td></tr>
<tr><td>response_templates</td><td>Pre-written response text per category + grievance type, with usage tracking (confirmed in Figma Administration &ndash; Response Templates)</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="note" style="margin-top:8pt;">Full column-level schema (types, constraints, keys) is available on request for engineering handoff &ndash; omitted here to keep this document at a reviewable length.</p>

<h2>3.2 API Design</h2>
<div class="note"><b>Current state, confirmed from the actual repos:</b> neither side is wired up yet. The frontend (<code>rajujha-ptn/Grievance-Management-Project</code>) has exactly two Next.js mock routes &ndash; <code>GET /api/dashboard</code> (returns static dashboard data with an artificial 800ms delay) and <code>GET/POST /api/grievances</code> (generic passthrough shape, not fully typed) &ndash; backed by <code>mockData.ts</code>, not a real database. The backend (<code>oan_grievance_service</code>) has an empty scaffold: <code>api/v1/__init__.py</code> exists, but the <code>grievance.py</code> module shown in the architecture diagram has not been written yet. Everything below is the target design, not yet-built fact.</div>

<h3>3.2.1 Access pattern</h3>
<p>Because the backend is a Frappe app, the API surface is not a hand-rolled REST layer &ndash; it uses Frappe's two built-in access patterns:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Resource REST</b> (<code>/api/resource/&lt;DocType&gt;</code>) &ndash; standard CRUD, permission-checked automatically against the caller's role and any custom permission query conditions. Good fit for simple reads (e.g. listing <code>Grievance Category</code> master data).</li>
<li><b>Whitelisted methods</b> (<code>/api/method/oan_grievance_service.api.v1.&lt;module&gt;.&lt;function&gt;</code>) &ndash; for anything that is a business action rather than a raw CRUD op (submit, respond, reassign, escalate, defer). This is where the actual workflow logic lives, and where RBAC/state-transition rules are enforced explicitly rather than relying on generic DocType permissions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Authentication is not part of this app's API surface at all &ndash; it's delegated to <code>oan_auth_service</code>'s JWT middleware (<code>validate_jwt_request</code>, referenced but not yet implemented per that repo's own scaffold).</p>

<h3>3.2.2 Whitelisted Method Catalogue (target)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Module</th><th>Method</th><th>Purpose</th><th>RBAC</th></tr>
<tr><td rowspan="4">grievance_management</td><td>submit_grievance</td><td>Create a grievance from any channel; validates, dedup-checks, generates ticket number, triggers routing</td><td>Farmer / Assisted-Submissions / any authenticated submitter</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>get_grievance</td><td>Full detail view, response-visibility-filtered per Appendix D-4</td><td>Owner, assigned officer, or admin</td></tr>
<tr><td>list_grievances</td><td>Work-queue search/filter/paginate</td><td>Scoped by role (own vs. assigned vs. cross-org)</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>confirm_resolution / reopen_grievance</td><td>Submitter confirms or reopens (mandatory reason) within the confirmation window</td><td>Owning submitter</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan="2">grievance_management (responses)</td><td>submit_response</td><td>Structured department response (Appendix D fields); response type drives next status</td><td>Assigned officer</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>get_responses</td><td>Response history, visibility-filtered (internal notes hidden from submitter)</td><td>Owner / officer / admin</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan="2">grievance_routing</td><td>request_reassignment</td><td>L1 requests reassignment with mandatory reason; target has no rights until approved</td><td>L1 Nodal Officer</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>decide_reassignment</td><td>Approve/reject; only commits target rights on approval</td><td>L2 Senior Nodal Officer, not the requester</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan="3">grievance_sla</td><td>request_sla_deferral</td><td>L1 requests extra days (reason &ge;20 chars); needs L2 approval unless self-approve is policy-permitted</td><td>L1 Nodal Officer</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>decide_sla_deferral</td><td>Approve/reject a deferral request</td><td>L2 Senior Nodal Officer / Department Head</td></tr>
<tr><td>escalate_grievance</td><td>Manual submitter escalation once SLA has elapsed</td><td>Owning submitter</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td rowspan="2">grievance_masters (admin config)</td><td>manage_category_assignment</td><td>Service category &rarr; department + L1/L2 officer + SLA days + auto-escalate + notify-on-submit</td><td>OAN Administrator</td></tr>
<tr><td>manage_sla_configuration</td><td>Global policy (max deferral, auto-escalate threshold) and per-category windows</td><td>OAN Administrator / Department Head (own dept)</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>grievance_notification</td><td>manage_notification_config</td><td>Per-event (EC-xxx) enable/disable, channel, recipients, template &ndash; confirmed as individually toggled, not grouped, per the Figma Notification Config screen</td><td>OAN Administrator</td></tr>
<tr><td>grievance_access_control</td><td>manage_users</td><td>User directory, role/region/status, deactivate/activate</td><td>OAN Administrator</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>grievance_management (reporting)</td><td>get_dashboard_data / get_analytics_data / export_report</td><td>Dashboard and Analytics &amp; Reporting screens (two distinct data sets, confirmed in Figma), CSV/PDF export</td><td>Any authenticated ops role, scope-filtered</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Idempotency-key support is needed on <code>submit_grievance</code> specifically, since it's the one call the offline-first mobile app can retry after a dropped connection &ndash; without it a retried sync could create a duplicate ticket.</p>

<!-- ============ 4. FRONTEND ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s4">4. Frontend Design &ndash; Figma-Verified Screens</h1>
<p>Confirmed against the actual Figma prototype ("Grievance Management Module"), not inferred from FRS text. Persistent left-rail navigation: Dashboard, All Grievances, Submit Grievance, Analytics &amp; Reporting, User Management, Administration, Settings &ndash; visibility RBAC-driven.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Screen</th><th>Key Elements (per Figma)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>KPI cards with period-over-period deltas; escalation alert with a link into the filtered work queue; submission/resolution trend; status distribution; category volume; recent grievances</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>All Grievances</td><td>Status summary cards; search + Advanced Filters; table (Ticket ID, Details, Category, Status, Priority, Submitted, Actions)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grievance Detail</td><td>Comments &amp; Communication thread (submission, each numbered department response tagged with its Response Type, internal notes visibly restricted to staff); "Dept Response (Appendix D)" / "Internal Note" tabs; SLA Tracker with Defer SLA; Case Management (status/officer/department/escalated); Submitter Details panel</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Submit Grievance</td><td>3 steps &ndash; Submitter Identity, Grievance Details (Grievance Type disabled until Service Category chosen), Review &amp; Submit (previews the ticket number pattern before submission); Save Draft / Save &amp; Continue throughout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Analytics &amp; Reporting</td><td>Distinct from Dashboard &ndash; Avg Resolution Time, Resolution Rate, Escalation Rate, Farmer Satisfaction (with response count), weekly trend, resolution rate by region, category volume, priority breakdown</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>User Management</td><td>Total/Active/Inactive counts; Team Members table with role, region, cases handled, status</td></tr>
<tr><td>Administration</td><td>Tabs: Nodal Officers (L1/L2), Category Assignments (department + L1/L2 officer + SLA days + auto-escalate + notify-on-submit), SLA Configuration (global policy + per-category windows), Notification Config (individually coded, individually toggled events), Response Templates (usage-tracked, "Copy to Response")</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Key frontend decisions:</b> server state via TanStack Query (not ad-hoc effects); the Submit wizard via React Hook Form + Zod, with Step 2's dependent dropdown matching the FR-02 dynamic-form requirement directly in the UI; the response form's fields swap based on Response Type rather than one form with many optional fields &ndash; the Figma's own "Dept Response (Appendix D)" tab label confirms this is understood, in the design itself, as a direct implementation of FRS Appendix D.</p>

<!-- ============ 5. RECONCILIATION ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s5">5. Figma Reconciliation Notes</h1>
<p>Found by comparing the actual prototype against the FRS/Scope-derived design. Concrete pre-build fix items, not documentation nitpicks.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Finding</th><th>Where</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Country code defaults to +255 (Tanzania), not +251 (Ethiopia)</b></td><td>Submit Grievance &ndash; Contact Mobile; Add Nodal Officer &ndash; Phone</td><td>Same wrong default in two unrelated screens confirms a shared phone-input component, not a typo. FRS &sect;3.11.8 anticipates exactly this: "any prototype sample value that conflicts with Ethiopia shall be corrected before release." Other screens in the same prototype correctly show +251, confirming that's the intended default everywhere.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Analytics &amp; Reporting is a distinct screen, not a Dashboard tab</td><td>Left nav has both as separate items</td><td>Design updated to reflect two screens with different metric sets (operational vs. performance/trend).</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SLA escalation threshold is a single 100% value, not 200%</b></td><td>Administration &ndash; SLA Configuration &ndash; "Auto-escalate Threshold (%)" = 100</td><td>The data design initially conflated the first-level threshold (100% = breach) with the FRS's separate "2&times; SLA" second-level escalation. Now modeled as two distinct fields.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><b>Notification config is per-event, not per-domain-event</b></td><td>Notification Config: individually coded, individually toggled rows (e.g. EC-003 "Auto-routing" shown Disabled while EC-004 "Manual Routing" is Active)</td><td>The Notification Service's enable/disable state must be keyed at the individual FRS-Appendix-C event level, even if the internal event bus groups related triggers more coarsely for routing.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Response Templates is a real entity</b></td><td>Administration &ndash; Response Templates: usage-tracked (used-count, last-used), "Copy to Response"</td><td>Added to the data design (&sect;3.1) &ndash; wasn't previously modeled.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><b>Service categories don't match the FRS &ndash; resolved, FRS wins</b></td><td>Frontend <code>GrievanceCategory</code> type (<code>mockData.ts</code>): Input, Schemes, Payments, Markets, Infrastructure, Other</td><td>FRS &sect;3.2.2 is explicit: <b>Inputs, Schemes, Payments, Credit, Markets</b>. Confirmed as authoritative &ndash; the frontend is what needs correcting (drop Infrastructure and Other, add Credit), not the other way around. Fix before <code>grievance_category</code> master data is finalized.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Status model mismatch &ndash; resolved, FRS wins</b></td><td>Frontend <code>GrievanceStatus</code> type: Assigned, Submitted, Pending Submit, In Progress, More Info Needed, Resolved, Rejected, <b>Under Review</b> &ndash; no separate Closed value</td><td>FRS &sect;3.11.3 confirmed as authoritative: Pending/Under Review/Resolved are <i>display groups</i> mapped onto canonical statuses, never statuses themselves &ndash; "Under Review" should not be a literal enum value. FR-06 confirms Closed is a real, separate canonical status from Resolved (the auto-close-after-cooling-period step). The frontend's status type needs fixing to match &sect;4.5's canonical list before it's wired to the real backend, or the two will disagree on what a grievance's status even is.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============ 6. INTEGRATIONS ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s6">6. External Integrations</h1>
<table>
<tr><th>System</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Failure Handling</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fayda ID</td><td>Synchronous verification during authentication</td><td>OTP fallback via registered mobile; DA-assisted manual verification as last resort</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td><b>GEN2 Registry</b></td><td>OAuth for Farmers, single sign-on for Development Agents &ndash; called once by the Mobile App at login, returns an identity token; not involved again for the rest of the session</td><td>Login blocked on failure &ndash; identity is audit-critical, no silent fallback. This is the actual system behind what earlier drafts of this document called "Farmer / DA Registry."</td></tr>
<tr><td>Beneficiary</td><td>Not called directly by this module. It's where a farmer sees their own registry data (profile, land records); GEN2 sources the identity claims it hands back from here.</td><td>N/A &ndash; out of scope for grievance flows, listed for architectural completeness only</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>SMS / Email</td><td>Asynchronous dispatch via Notification Service queue</td><td>Retry with backoff; failures surface on an ops "failed notifications" view</td></tr>
<tr><td>IVR / Helpline</td><td>Transcription or live-agent intake through the same grievance API as any other channel</td><td>Degrades to live-agent path if transcription fails</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Telegram</td><td>Bot webhook &rarr; same grievance API</td><td>Conversation state held server-side so a bot restart doesn't lose in-flight submissions</td></tr>
<tr><td>ATI / MoA Systems</td><td>Bidirectional for routed grievances</td><td>Falls back to email-based structured response where no API integration exists yet</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============ 7. SECURITY / NFR ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s7">7. Security, RBAC and Non-Functional Summary</h1>
<ul>
<li><b>Encryption</b> at rest and in transit; encrypted identity linkage retained even for approved-anonymous grievances.</li>
<li><b>RBAC enforcement</b> is centralized (one authorization check shared by every service), not re-implemented per endpoint.</li>
<li><b>Immutable audit</b> &ndash; append-only tables, no application-level delete path.</li>
<li><b>File safety</b> &ndash; every attachment virus-scanned before being linked to a case.</li>
<li><b>PII minimization</b> &ndash; approved-anonymous cases strip submitter identity at the query layer for department-facing views, not just in the UI.</li>
<li><b>Performance</b> &ndash; submission response &le;10s; SLA batch job &le;30 min; reporting reads from projections, not live OLTP.</li>
<li><b>Reliability</b> &ndash; 99.5% uptime target; offline-capable mobile app with idempotent sync.</li>
<li><b>Localization</b> &ndash; Amharic and English as first-class locales throughout, UI and notifications alike.</li>
<li><b>Scale</b> &ndash; ticket sequences partitioned per region/woreda/category to avoid single-counter contention at national scale.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ============ 8. RISKS ============ -->
<h1 class="section" id="s8">8. Risks, Assumptions and Open Questions</h1>

<div class="note"><b>Resolved: scope is Ethiopia-only for now.</b> Three internal architecture documents from the <code>oan_grievance_service</code> repo (<code>administrative_area_tree_implementation_plan.md</code>, <code>verifier_management_schema_and_flow.md</code>, <code>verifier_routing_and_escalation_paths.md</code>) describe a considerably more general, multi-country platform. Confirmed: current direction is the Ethiopia-scoped FRS v1.3 implementation this design follows &ndash; the multi-country architecture is a possible future direction, not current scope. The specific conflicts below are kept on record so they don't get silently reintroduced by code written against those architecture docs:
<table style="margin-top:8pt;">
<tr><th>Area</th><th>FRS / this design</th><th>Actual architecture docs</th></tr>
<tr><td>Geography</td><td>Fixed Region/Zone/Woreda/Kebele fields</td><td>Generic Nested Set tree (<code>Administrative Area</code>), explicitly built for multi-country (Ethiopia, Kenya, Nigeria, India fixtures named)</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Roles</td><td>Farmer, L1/L2 Nodal Officer, Department Head, OAN Administrator</td><td>Case Officer, Supervisor, Nodal Officer &ndash; different names and hierarchy, rank derived from tree position</td></tr>
<tr><td>SLA model</td><td>One clock, 50/80/100/200% thresholds</td><td>Five separate milestone timestamps (first response 4h, periodic update every 48h, remand directive 24h, resolution 10 days, appeal expiry)</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Submitter confirmation</td><td>7-day window, reopen returns to same department</td><td>15-day appeal window; appeal bypasses the original officer entirely, routes to the parent-area supervisor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Features not in FRS at all</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>Leave/inactivity two-tier fallback routing, Supervisor Remand-with-directives, Master-Child cluster linking for bulk/related incidents</td></tr>
</table>
Anyone building against the <code>oan_grievance_service</code> architecture docs directly should build to the FRS's role model, SLA model, and 7-day confirmation window instead &ndash; the two are not reconcilable by small tweaks, so code written to the wrong one would need real rework, not a patch.</div>

<table style="margin-top:14pt;">
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fayda ID, SMS gateway, email, IVR/helpline, ATI/MoA system availability</td><td>Dependency readiness</td><td>Track in readiness assessments; design assumes graceful degradation but can't substitute for these being live</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Exact SLA days, routing rules, escalation authority per category</td><td>Configuration TBD</td><td>Modeled as admin-configurable; values needed from ATI/MoA before Development Milestone 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ticket number category codes (INP/SCH/PAY/MAR/CRD)</td><td>Confirmation needed</td><td>Prototype's codes vs. the FRS's own worked example differ; confirm the definitive list with ATI/MoA</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Telegram integration scope</td><td>Deliverable clarification</td><td>Ticket creation only, or full lifecycle management? Bot conversation flows need FRS-level detail</td></tr>
<tr><td>ATI/MoA integration depth</td><td>Integration risk</td><td>Structured response ingestion via API, or email-only fallback? Design provides the fallback either way</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>Second-level (2&times; SLA) escalation authority per category</td><td>Configuration TBD</td><td>Must be populated before go-live or second-level escalation has no recipient</td></tr>
<tr><td>+255 country-code default</td><td>Pre-build fix</td><td>See &sect;5 &ndash; needs fixing in the shared phone-input component before build</td></tr>
</table>

